// Fixture: numeric_coercion_narrowing_rejected
// Rule under test: GDDL Spec v4 §5 -- "narrowing with fractional loss is a
// compile-time error." Two sources of a genuinely fractional value, both stored into
// integer-typed fields: the result of a division that doesn't divide evenly, and a
// float literal with a real fractional part. Confirms rejection isn't silent
// truncation (e.g. `10 / 3` quietly becoming `3`) and applies uniformly regardless of
// how the fractional value was produced.

define Stats
	count = i32
	small = u8

Stats FromDivision
	count = 10 / 3
	// 10 / 3 = 3.333... -- genuine fractional loss if stored into an i32 field.
	// ERROR expected: must be rejected at compile time, not silently truncated to 3.

Stats FromLiteral
	small = 3.5
	// bare float literal with a real fractional part, stored into a u8 field.
	// ERROR expected: same rejection, from a literal rather than a computed value.

// Expected (prediction, pending golden output from Compiler Core reference run):
//   Two independent compile errors (per-instance, matching the established
//   independent-validation pattern -- see initialization/init_uninitialized_only_caught_at_export.gddl
//   for the precedent of multiple independent errors in one fixture):
//   - FromDivision: field `count` -- narrowing with fractional loss (10/3 = 3.333...)
//     rejected. Phase 6 (Resolve), check "numeric_coercion" (per the phase/check
//     established in an earlier golden batch's _meta note).
//   - FromLiteral: field `small` -- narrowing with fractional loss (3.5) rejected.
//     Same phase/check as above.
//   Neither instance should silently truncate to 3 or 3 respectively -- that would be
//   the exact failure mode this fixture exists to catch.
